# DURABLE REPRO — a comprehension over a NESTED `Vector` SEGFAULTS, with no
# sigil anywhere in the program.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg, C backend): `gg check` reports "OK: no
# semantic errors", `gg build` succeeds, and the binary exits **139 (SIGSEGV)**
# with no output.
#
# ⚠ `gg run` MASKS this as exit 1 with no output — reproduce by BUILDING and
# running the binary directly, or it looks like an ordinary build error.
#
# ⚠ NOT SIGIL-RELATED, and that is the whole point of this fixture. It was
# first surfaced while probing `&`-iterable element write-through, where the
# sigil spellings crashed and the natural conclusion was that `&` caused it.
# Measured across all four spellings, every one `gg check`-clean:
#
#   [x.len() for x in a]          -> SIGSEGV 139   <- THIS FIXTURE, no sigils
#   [x.len() for x in &a]         -> SIGSEGV 139
#   [grow(&x) for x in a]         -> SIGSEGV 139
#   [grow(&x) for x in &a]        -> SIGSEGV 139
#
# The ALL-BARE form crashing is what proves the sigil is incidental: this is a
# pre-existing comprehension-over-nested-`Vector` defect. Do not fold it into
# the `&`-write-through family; a fix there will not touch it.
#
# ⚠ It is also a triage-hazard case study. An earlier probe of a DIFFERENT
# comprehension shape (`[bumpv(&e) for e in &a]` with a `void` element
# function) exited 0 with the write merely lost, so the crash looked
# unreproducible; the two shapes differ in whether the element expression
# produces a value. When a crash claim does not reproduce, vary the shape
# before concluding it was wrong.
#
# INTENDED: the comprehension evaluates `x.len()` per element and the program
# prints `1`.
#
# ORACLE (ggdef): the element expression is a builtin method call on a nested
# collection — record the verdict when the shape enters the subset. A
# value-semantics interpreter cannot observe the memory fault either way
# (Core #13); ASan on the real backends is the instrument.
#
# The vector is built from computed elements, not a static literal, so no
# const-fold path can elide the allocation.
#
# Cited from TODO.md (Backend / codegen).

int seed():
    return 1

void main():
    Vector[int] inner0 = [seed()]
    Vector[int] inner1 = [seed() + 1]
    Vector[Vector[int]] a = [inner0, inner1]
    Vector[int] r = [x.len() for x in a]
    print(r.get(0).unwrap())
